# Brent with derivatives

`BrentDerivative` minimizes a function of one scalar variable on a finite
interval using objective values and first derivatives. It combines secant
steps on the derivative with a fallback step guided by the derivative's sign.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/brent_derivative/struct.BrentDerivative.html)

  | Property    | Requirement                                              |
  | ----------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |
  | Objective   | A differentiable, deterministic scalar function           |
  | Evaluations | `CostFunction` and `Gradient` with a scalar derivative     |
  | Search      | Local interval reduction, ideally on a unimodal function  |
  | Constraints | Scalar box bounds with finite `lower < upper`             |
  | Parameters  | Scalar `f32` or `f64`, without optional features           |

## When to use it

Consider this method when an interval isolates a minimum and a reliable first
derivative is inexpensive. The derivative can guide the search more directly
than objective values alone and supplies an optional stationarity test. It
does not require a second derivative.

Compare [Brent](../brent/) when derivatives are expensive or unreliable, and
[golden-section search](../golden-section/) for a simple method using only
objective comparisons. All three depend on the interval: multiple minima,
noise, or misleading derivative signs can undermine the search. They do not
certify a global minimum on a multimodal objective.

## How it works

Basin follows the `dbrent` routine in [Press et al.
(1992)](#ref-press-etal-1992), §10.3, the derivative-using companion to
[Brent's minimization method (1973)](#ref-brent-1973).

The solver retains a best point and two other samples, each with its cost
and derivative. It forms secant estimates for a zero of the derivative and
accepts a proposal only when it lies inside the current interval, points
downhill according to the current derivative, and satisfies a step-size
safeguard.

If no proposal passes, it moves halfway from the retained best point toward
the endpoint indicated by the derivative's sign. A positive derivative sends
the search left; a negative derivative sends it right. Each evaluated cost
then updates the interval and retained history. Minimum trial displacements
use the position tolerances.

## Example

The function `x^3 - 3x` has its minimum on `[0, 2]` at `1`, with cost `-2`.
Its scalar derivative is `3x^2 - 3`. The example supplies both evaluations,
declares scalar box bounds, and enables an absolute gradient tolerance.

<!-- compile: solver-brent-derivative -->
```rust
use basin::{
    BoxBounds, BrentDerivative, ConstraintDescription, ConstraintSet,
    Constraints, CostFunction, Executor, FirstOrderState, Gradient,
    Termination,
};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct Cubic {
    lower: f64,
    upper: f64,
}

impl CostFunction for Cubic {
    type Param = f64;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &f64) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok(x.powi(3) - 3.0 * x)
    }
}

impl Gradient for Cubic {
    type Gradient = f64;

    fn gradient(&self, x: &f64) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok(3.0 * x * x - 3.0)
    }
}

impl Constraints for Cubic {
    type Set = ConstraintSet<BoxBounds<f64>>;

    fn constraints(&self) -> <Self::Set as ConstraintDescription>::Borrowed<'_> {
        ConstraintSet::new().with_bounds(BoxBounds::new(
            Some(&self.lower),
            Some(&self.upper),
        ))
    }
}

fn main() {
    let problem = Cubic { lower: 0.0, upper: 2.0 };
    let solver = BrentDerivative::new()
        .with_absolute_gradient_tolerance(1e-8);
    let result = Executor::new(problem, solver, FirstOrderState::new(0.5))
        .max_iter(100)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    assert!(matches!(result.report.termination, Termination::Converged(_)));
    assert!((result.best_param() - 1.0).abs() < 1e-6);
    assert!((result.best_cost() + 2.0).abs() < 1e-10);
    assert_eq!(result.counts.cost_evals, result.counts.gradient_evals);
}
```

The derivative in `FirstOrderState` belongs to the current probe. Use
`best_param()` and `best_cost()` for the lowest recorded objective; the most
recent probe need not improve it.

## Practical considerations

### Declare the interval on the problem

The complete constraint declaration must be `ConstraintSet<BoxBounds<F>>`
with both finite endpoints present and `lower < upper`. Linear and nonlinear
constraint blocks are unsupported. Keep interval arithmetic in range and
return finite costs and derivatives at all sampled points.

Initialization clamps the seed into the interval and moves an endpoint seed
to a golden-section interior point. A boundary minimum can be approached even
when its derivative is nonzero. If the interval is unknown,
[`MinimumBracketer`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/bracket/minimum/struct.MinimumBracketer.html)
can search using costs alone; see the [Brent example](../brent/#example).
Check its success before using the outer endpoints as bounds and its middle
point as the seed.

### Separate position accuracy from stationarity

The native interval test matches Brent's:
`abs(x - m) + (b - a) / 2 <= 2 * tol`, where `x` is the retained best point,
`m = (a + b) / 2`, and `tol = relative * abs(x) + absolute`.
`with_absolute_position_tolerance` defaults to `1e-12`, and
`with_relative_position_tolerance` defaults to `sqrt(ε)` for the scalar type.
Both must be finite and strictly positive; invalid settings panic. They also
set the minimum trial displacement.

`with_absolute_gradient_tolerance` optionally tests `abs(f'(x))` at the
current published point. It is disabled by default and combines with the
native test using OR. `None` disables this optional check, and zero requests
an exact-zero derivative. A small derivative can also occur at a maximum or
on a flat region; it is not sufficient evidence of a minimum by itself.

At an active bound, the correct constrained solution can have a nonzero
derivative. The gradient check uses the ordinary derivative rather than a
projected stationarity measure, so use the interval test when a boundary
solution is possible. Optional cost-change and step-change tests also combine
with OR and may stop before the interval has reached its requested width.

### Account for both evaluations

Initialization and each evaluated trial request `Gradient::cost_and_gradient`.
Its default implementation calls `cost` and `gradient` separately. Override
it when they share substantial work; the accounting still charges one cost
and one gradient request per combined evaluation.

Set iteration and evaluation limits on the executor. Read `result.report`
before treating a returned point as converged. A run can stop because of a
budget, an optional tolerance, native interval convergence, or a numerical
stall when no distinct point remains inside the interval.

## Implementation

`FirstOrderState<F, F>` publishes a matching scalar point, cost, and derivative
at each evaluated trial. The solver keeps its interpolation history and
interval separately. A fresh run resets that history; an exact
solver-and-state checkpoint preserves it and the evaluation counts.

Scalar `f32` and `f64` require no linear-algebra backend or optional feature
and work on WASM. Choose position and derivative tolerances consistent with
the scalar type and objective scaling.

